    {
      q: 'What is co-citation?',
      a: 'Two papers are co-cited when a third paper cites both. The more often that happens, the more the field treats them as related.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What is bibliographic coupling?',
      a: 'Two papers are coupled when they cite the same sources. It links recent papers well, because it doesn’t need them to have been cited yet.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which tools draw literature maps?',
      a: 'Connected Papers, ResearchRabbit and Litmaps build maps from online databases, each with its own free and paid limits. PaperLab draws a graph of the papers already in your library, on your machine.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How many seed papers should I start with?',
      a: 'Three to five strong, well-cited papers usually give a stable map. Too few and the map follows one author’s view; too many and it loses focus.',
    },
