  <h2>The four links that matter</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Link</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Backward citation</td><td>What a paper builds on: its foundations</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Forward citation</td><td>Who built on it since: where the field went</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href={href('glossary/co-citation/')}>Co-citation</a></td><td>Papers the field reads together</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href={href('glossary/bibliographic-coupling/')}>Bibliographic coupling</a></td><td>Recent papers working on the same problem</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A mapping routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Pick three to five seed papers.</li>
    <li>Collect their references and the papers citing them.</li>
    <li>Keep papers that several seeds point to: they're central.</li>
    <li>Look at clusters, and name each one in a sentence.</li>
    <li>Read one or two papers per cluster before going deeper.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Reading the map</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Big, central nodes</strong> are foundations: cite them, don't necessarily read them first.</li>
    <li><strong>Bridges between clusters</strong> are often the most interesting papers.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent nodes with few links</strong> may be new directions, or noise.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
